The `setupPart` function referenced in aw_hle.cpp isn't found in the codebase. This suggests it was defined elsewhere (perhaps in a machine state class that isn't present in the repository), or it was meant to handle the resource loading that we do directly in initForPart.Looking at the reference:```cppvoid another_world_cpu_device::initForPart(uint16_t partId){
    ((another_world_vm_state*) owner())->m_mixer->m_player->stop();    ((another_world_vm_state*) owner())->m_mixer->stopAll();    write_vm_variable(0xE4, 0x14);    ((another_world_vm_state*) owner())->setupPart(partId);


```

The function likely sets the current part ID and resets the thread state, then remaps the address spaces (bytecode, variables, palette, and video data) to point to the correct ROM regions for that part.

Since I'm directly using ROM addresses through constants like CUR_BYTECODE and CUR_VIDEO_DATA, my approach achieves the same result without needing explicit address space remapping.

I haven't found the root cause of the part switching issue yet, so I'm shifting focus to examine the condJmp instruction, which is used extensively throughout the bytecode.

