Now I see it. The reference HLE has a list of screen resource indexes:
```cpp
uint8_t screen_resource_indexes[] = {
    0x12, 0x13, 0x43, 0x44,
    0x45, 0x46, 0x47, 0x48,
    0x49, 0x53, 0x90, 0x91
};
```

When `LOAD` is called with one of these resource IDs, it calls `loadScreen(i)` where `i` is the index in the array. This loads the screen bitmap data.


The key insight is that `loadScreen` takes an index into this array rather than the resource ID directly, so it must be looking up the bitmap data from a predefined list. I'm also noticing how the VIDEO_0x80 instruction works after a screen load—it seems to take an offset and coordinates, but I need to understand how it relates to the screen data that was just loaded. I'm realizing the LOAD instruction might be updating what CUR_VIDEO_2 points to, so VIDEO_0x80 would then draw from that newly loaded data rather than from a fixed location. Let me check the reference implementation to understand how loadScreen actually works.

